{
  "abstract": "DomainTheory is a single Lean 4 / Mathlib library (v4.36.0-rc2, lake build green, no sorry) assembled from five Palomar-registered formalizations vendored under vendor/ for provenance (scott1972, scott1976, scott1980, scott1982, scott_models; see PROVENANCE.md and vendor/FROZEN.txt). The working Lean is the golfed DomainTheory/ tree: ContinuousLattice ← 1972, DataTypesAsLattices ← 1976, NeighbourhoodSystems ← 1980, InformationSystems ← 1982, BridgeTheorems ← scott_models. Comparator (Challenge.lean / Solution.lean / comparator.json) validates (i) the cross-presentation bridges formerly registered as scott_models (1972 continuous-lattice / 1980 neighbourhood / 1982 information-system isomorphisms, including D ≃o RoundFilter ≃o RoundInfoSysElement and the S-expression equation |T| ≃o |A + (T × T)|), and (ii) the selected Data Types as Lattices (1976) declarations formerly registered as scott1976. The golfed library preserves those validated results under Mathlib-style names. Distribution today is the public GitHub repository only; a CMU-CS technical report (CMU-CS-TR) is planned. Author Contributions: Lars Warren Ericson — primary formalization engineer (all Lean 4, AI-assisted proof architecture, repository assembly); Dana S. Scott — author of the original source mathematics (1972–1982), project sponsorship, historical context, and high-level advisory oversight of formalization scope; Vijay D'Silva and Brian Milnes — project initiation, pre-submission review, and advisory feedback on repository formatting and technical report generation. Source-paper authorship is distinct from combined-library co-authorship; Scott, D'Silva, and Milnes did not write the Lean.",
